answers.approach:
(sung slow, rocking)

Hush, little graph, you are frayed at the seam;
one key for every door was a beautiful dream.

We'll carry the doubt and we'll say how we're sure,
and let the cost of being wrong pick the door.

Consent rides the edge, not a box at the end;
it ages, it wanders, it breaks — and we mend.

So sleep, little graph, we won't get this right;
we'll just be less wrong — and we'll know it — tonight.

(Plainly, under the tune: I'd stop chasing a universal ID, because the obvious answer — pick the winning identifier, join everything to it — is the thing that keeps failing. Instead: one probabilistic graph, every edge carrying a calibrated confidence and a consent state with a lifespan, and a different threshold per decision. Frequency capping can run on a 0.6 match; a personalized creative naming a product someone bought cannot. Then measure identity error's effect on outcomes, not on match rate. Expect to hit the wall on calibration — there's no honest ground truth for "same person," so your confidence scores are guesses about guesses — and on consent drift, which moves faster than any graph refresh.)
